4.3 Jacobian independence and graded substitution
We include the short algebraic steps because the coefficient-uniform survival
claim depends on them.
Lemma 4.2 (Characteristic-zero Jacobian criterion). If G1, . . . , Gr ∈
K[X1, . . . , Xr] have nonzero Jacobian determinant, then they are alge-
braically independent over K.
Proof. Suppose a nonzero relation H(G1, . . . , Gr) = 0 has least total degree.
Differentiation yields
JT
G HY1 (G), . . . , HYr (G)
T
= 0.
Over the fraction field the Jacobian matrix is invertible, so every HYi (G)
vanishes. Minimality says that a nonzero derivative of smaller degree cannot
vanish at G. Hence all derivatives of H are zero polynomials. In character-
istic zero, H is constant, a contradiction.
Applying Lemma 4.2 to G = ∇PE0 and using Lemma 4.1 shows that
every positive face-gradient tuple is algebraically independent. The criterion
itself is classical; its role here is the typed transfer through successive shear
phases.
Lemma 4.3 (Associated-graded injection). Let A be a filtered K-algebra,
and suppose Zi = in(Qi) ∈ gr A are algebraically independent. Then
K[X1, . . . , Xr] −→ gr A, Xi 7−→ Zi, (4.5)
is injective. If H1, . . . , Hr are algebraically independent, then so are
H1(Z), . . . , Hr(Z).
